nodes.statement:
For all arrival rates $\lambda$, job sizes $S$, and numbers of servers $k\geq 2$,
    with the SEK-SMOD parameterization given in 3.5,
    if M/G/$k$/SRPT is positive recurrent,
    the mean response time of SEK-SMOD is smaller than that of SRPT-$k$:
    \begin{align*}
        \mathbb{E}[T^{SEK \hy SMOD}] < \mathbb{E}[T^{SRPT \hy k}]. 
    \end{align*}

With the constants $c_1, c_2 > 0$ defined in 3.5 that depend on $\lambda, S, k$ and not on $\epsilon$, such that for any SEK-SMOD parameterization as in 5.2 and any divergence starting state $\boldsymbol{b}\boldsymbol{b}$,
    \begin{align}

        P(\textsc{Bad}_t \mid \boldsymbol{b}\boldsymbol{b}(t) = \boldsymbol{b}\boldsymbol{b}) &\le c_1 \epsilon,    \\

        \mathbb{E}[\Delta_t \mid\boldsymbol{b}\boldsymbol{b}(t) = \boldsymbol{b}\boldsymbol{b} \,\&\, \textsc{Bad}_t] &\ge - c_2 \epsilon.
    \end{align}

For the SEK-SMOD parameterization given in 3.5
    and any allowed starting state $\boldsymbol{b}\boldsymbol{b}$,
    all neutral and good scenarios achieve nonnegative $\Delta_t$:
    \begin{align*}
        [\Delta_t \mid \boldsymbol{b}\boldsymbol{b}(t) = \boldsymbol{b}\boldsymbol{b} \,\&\, (\textsc{Good}_t \wedge \textsc{Neutral}_t)] \ge 0, \text{always.}
    \end{align*}

With the constants $c_3, c_4 > 0$ defined in 3.5 that depend on $\lambda, S, k$ and not on $\epsilon$, and any divergence starting state $\boldsymbol{b}\boldsymbol{b}$,
    \begin{align*}
        P(\textsc{Good}_t \mid \boldsymbol{b}\boldsymbol{b}(t) = \boldsymbol{b}\boldsymbol{b}) &\ge c_3, \qquad
        E[\Delta_t \mid \boldsymbol{b}\boldsymbol{b}(t) = \boldsymbol{b}\boldsymbol{b} \,\&\, \textsc{Good}_t] \ge c_4 \epsilon.
    \end{align*}

In the SMOD-SRPT joint system,
    under an arbitrary sequence of arrivals,
    for any pair of times $t_1 \le t_2$,
    the positive part at time $t_1$ upper bounds the positive part at time $t_2$:
    \begin{align*}
        r^+(t_1) \ge r^+(t_2).
    \end{align*}

If at time $t_1$, SMOD dominates SRPT-$k$, then for all $t_2 \ge t_1$,
    SMOD dominates SRPT-$k$.
